# 5.8.2
- Bugfix `recordSwap()`: a user-supplied `risk` was assigned to the wrong households
  if `data` was not sorted by `hid`. `data` is sorted internally, but the rows of
  `risk` were left in their original order, so that mostly households without risk
  were swapped and households at risk were not. The risk values now follow their
  households. Results for data sorted by `hid` are unchanged.
- Bugfix `recordSwap()`: `risk` given as a column name did not work if the column
  itself was called `risk` (error, or a wrong column used as risk).
- Bugfix `localSuppression()` / `kAnon()` for data frames with `combs`: the subsets of
  key variables and the `importance` vector were addressed by column position, which
  is only correct if the key variables are the leading columns of the data. Otherwise
  a user-supplied `importance` was applied to the wrong variables, and with
  `strataVars` wrong columns were processed (too few suppressions although
  `anonymity = TRUE` was reported, or an error). The subsets are now formed over the
  key variables wherever they are in the data. Results are unchanged if the key
  variables are the leading columns, and for `sdcMicroObj` objects.
- `createSdcObj()` works without categorical key variables (#91): `keyVars` is now
  optional, e.g. `createSdcObj(Tarragona, numVars = c("SALES", "NET.PROFIT"))`; at least
  one of `keyVars` and `numVars` must be given. For such an object no frequency counts
  and no risk measures for categorical key variables are computed (`risk$individual`
  and `risk$global` are `NULL`). The methods for numerical variables
  (`microaggregation()`, `mafast()`, `microaggrGower()`, `addNoise()`, `rankSwap()`,
  `shuffle()`, `topBotCoding()`, `dRisk()`, `dUtility()`, `dRiskRMD()`), `pram()` on
  pram variables, `undolast()`, `extractManipData()`, `writeSafeFile()` and `report()`
  work; `show()` and `print()` leave out the sections on categorical key variables.
  Methods that need categorical key variables (`localSuppression()`, `kAnon()`,
  `localSupp()`, `globalRecode()`, `groupAndRename()`, `varToFactor()`,
  `varToNumeric()`, `ldiversity()`, `LocalRecProg()`, `suda2()`, `riskyCells()`,
  `modRisk()`, `measure_risk()`, `freq()`, `kAnon_violations()`, `addGhostVars()`,
  `AI_applyAnonymization()`) stop with a clear message. Objects with categorical key
  variables behave as before.
- `sdcApp()`: an SDC problem can be set up with numerical key variables only (#91).
  The summary page, the side bar and the generated script leave out everything on
  categorical key variables; the pages that need them (linked variables, recoding,
  k-anonymity, suppression by risk, the categorical risk and result pages, AI-assisted
  anonymization) show a notice instead. PRAM and the methods and results for numerical
  variables work as usual.
- Bugfix `recordSwap()` (#333): with a user-supplied `risk`, all households with risk 0
  at a hierarchy level shared one entry in the internal donor list, so that exactly one
  of them, always the last household of the data, could still be chosen as donor. In
  the example of the issue this household was swapped although its risk was 0.
  Households with risk 0 at a hierarchy level are now never donors at this level, as
  documented ("only households with risk set to 0 are not swapped"); a household at
  risk without a suitable donor is reported in the log file as before. Give non-risky
  households a small positive risk to allow them as donors.
- Bugfix `recordSwap()`: if the number of swaps to distribute over the lowest hierarchy
  level was 0 (e.g. `swaprate = 0`), the first group nevertheless received one swap
  with probability 1 / (number of groups). The same happened with `swaprate > 0`
  whenever the swaps divided evenly among the groups (e.g. four regions of 100
  households and `swaprate = 0.1`: 42 instead of 40 swapped households in about a third
  of the runs); the swap rate is now met exactly there. All other results with
  `swaprate > 0` are unchanged.
- `recordSwap()`: the documentation of `risk` and `return_swapped_id` states that risk
  values are donor sampling weights and that donors are flagged as swapped, too.
- `addNoise(method = "ROMM")` is reimplemented (#271, thanks to Øyvind Langsrud for the
  report and the QR formulation, cf. his R package RegSDC). **Results change:** the
  perturbed data now have exactly the same sample means and the same sample covariance
  matrix as the original data; previously the means were not preserved. The method no
  longer builds and orthonormalizes an n x n matrix, so it needs O(n p^2) time instead
  of O(n^3) and no n x n memory (4580 x 3 values: well below a second). The meaning and
  the default of `p` are kept (`p = 0`: no change). Rows with missing values are returned
  unchanged, with a warning (previously the whole result was `NA`); more complete rows
  than variables are required; constant and collinear variables are handled.
- `pram()`: the documentation of `pd` and `alpha` now describes what is computed (#347).
  `pd` is the lower bound of the diagonal of the *initial* matrix `P`; the matrix
  that is applied is the invariant matrix `Rs = alpha * R + (1 - alpha) * I`, whose
  diagonal can be smaller than `pd`. Its rows sum to 1, its columns in general do not;
  invariance means `p' Rs = p'` for the observed frequencies `p`. The behaviour is
  unchanged. A new Details section explains the construction.
- `pram()`: a user-supplied transition matrix is now accepted if its row sums equal 1
  up to numerical tolerance (`sqrt(.Machine$double.eps)`). Previously the check used
  `.Machine$double.eps`, so valid matrices (e.g. with entries `1/49`) could be rejected
  because of floating-point rounding.
- Bugfix: `microaggrGower()` passed the label of the last level of each ordered
  factor to `VIM::gowerD()` instead of the number of levels. This failed with
  VIM >= 7.5.1, which validates the argument, and with earlier VIM versions gave
  wrong distances for ordered factors whose labels are not `"1"`, ..., `"n"`.
- `rankSwap()`: the documentation of `P` now matches the implementation. `P` is the
  rank range as a *proportion* of the swappable records (`P = 0.1` for a 10% rank
  range), not a percentage of the total sample size; swappable records exclude
  missing values and the values grouped by `TopPercent`/`BottomPercent`. `P > 1`
  (which does not restrict the rank range at all) now raises a warning, negative
  `P` an error. Thanks to the user who reported the mismatch.
- Bugfix (sdcApp): rank swapping with method `P` multiplied the slider value by
  100, so e.g. 0.95 was passed as `P = 95` and records were swapped without any
  rank restriction. The slider value is now passed unchanged.
- `valTable()`: the default swapping range for method `"swappNum"` is now
  `p = 0.15` (previously `p = 15`, i.e. an unrestricted rank range).
- `AI_applyAnonymization()` gains early stopping of the refinement loop via the new
  `tol` and `patience` arguments: refinement now terminates once the combined utility
  score U stops improving by more than `tol` for `patience` consecutive iterations,
  instead of always running all `max_iter` rounds.
- `query_llm()` now omits the `temperature` parameter for OpenAI GPT-5.x and o-series
  reasoning models (which do not accept a custom temperature), enabling use of e.g. `gpt-5.5`.
- Bugfix: `ldiversity()` now computes distinct l-diversity measure correctly in case of `NAs` in Keyvars
  * improved/simplified uderlying C++ code
  * added Unit-Tests for `ldiversity()`
- Fixing some Header-Definitions for CRAN-Compliance
- Updated Unit-Tests for `pram()`

# 5.8.1
- New AI-assisted anonymization features:
  + `AI_createSdcObj()`: LLM-assisted variable classification into SDC roles
    (key variables, numerical variables, PRAM variables, weight, household ID)
  + `AI_applyAnonymization()`: Agentic loop that proposes, evaluates, and refines
    anonymization strategies using structured tool calling
  + Provider-agnostic LLM access via `query_llm()` supporting OpenAI, Anthropic,
    and any OpenAI-compatible endpoint (Ollama, Azure, vLLM)
  + Privacy by design: only metadata (variable names, types, cardinality, factor
    levels) is sent to the LLM — never the actual microdata
  + Combined utility score for quantitative strategy comparison
  + New vignette: "AI-Assisted Statistical Disclosure Control with sdcMicro"
- AI integration in `sdcApp()`:
  + New "AI-Assisted" tab for interactive strategy generation and comparison
  + "AI suggest variables" button in the setup panel with auto-fill
  + Applied AI methods tracked in the Reproducibility tab
- Renamed function prefixes from `KI_` to `AI_` for consistency
- Remove `shinyBS` dependency; replaced with pure R+JS implementations
- Fix deprecated enum-to-float arithmetic warnings in C++ code (`RankSwapping.h`, `Mdav.h`) flagged by clang 21+ and gcc 14+

